      "localisation": 0.7,
      "remedy": 0.6666666666666666,
      "component_match": true,
      "file_hit": false,
      "file_f1": 0.0,
      "symbol_hit": true,
      "root_cause": "The post-upgrade hook tries to reach the Keystone admin API, which is intentionally not running while the unit is paused. This causes the `openstack-upgrade` action to fail when the unit is paused.",
      "proposed_fix": "Modify the post-upgrade hook to check if the unit is paused before attempting to reach the Keystone admin API. If the unit is paused, skip the domain-backend update and mark it as pending for when the unit resumes.",
      "root_cause_rationale": "Correctly identifies the mechanism: while the unit is paused, the local Keystone admin API is intentionally down, and the post-upgrade hook's attempt to reach it causes the openstack-upgrade action to fail. However, it is imprecise: it omits the specific path through domain_backend_changed()/create_or_show_domain() over domain-backend relations and the missing is_unit_paused_set() guard.",
      "fix_rationale": "The proposed fix is plausible and in the right area: check whether the unit is paused before attempting the Keystone admin API call and skip the domain-backend update if so. However, it targets the post-upgrade hook rather than the actual missing guard in domain_backend_changed()/create_or_show_domain(), and the 'mark pending' behavior is not grounded in the ground truth.",
      "ground_truth_root_cause": "The keystone charm's config-changed-postupgrade hook (run by the openstack-upgrade action)\ncalls update_all_domain_backends(), which calls domain_backend_changed() for every\ndomain-backend relation (e.g. keystone-ldap). domain_backend_changed() unconditionally calls\ncreate_or_show_domain(), which queries the local Keystone admin API through manager.py.\nUnlike the charm's other identity-relation hooks, domain_backend_changed() had no\nis_unit_paused_set() guard around that call. When the documented paused-single-unit upgrade\nprocedure pauses the keystone unit before running the openstack-upgrade action, the local\nKeystone API (served by apache2 on the admin port) is down, so the API call raises a\nConnectionRefusedError/RuntimeError and the hook crashes, breaking the procedure whenever a\ndomain-backend relation is present.",
